    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d165">

                <pb facs="0694" n="663" xml:id="pg663"/>

                <head>165. Memorandum From the President’s Deputy Assistant (Friedersdorf) to President Ford<anchor n="1" xml:id="d165-f1"/></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName>Washington</placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1975-07-12">July 12,
                    1975</date>.</dateline></opener>
                <list subtype="subject" type="description">
                    <label>SUBJECT</label>
                    <item>
                        Solzhenitsyn
                    </item>
                </list>
                <p>I am concerned about the Solzhenitsyn
                    issue and its impact on the right wing on the Hill.</p>
                <p>One possible solution might be to host a meeting, a luncheon or some other type
                    of event as soon as possible for Solzhenitsyn, Nureyev, and Rostropovich.<anchor n="2"
                        xml:id="d165-f2"/></p>
                <p>Both Nureyev and Rostropovich are now appearing in the Kennedy Center before sell
                    out crowds and both are highly publicized exiles but not nearly as
                    controversial, of course, as Solzhenitsyn.</p>
                <p>They are all three artists of great talent and the meeting could be held as an
                    artistic and intellectual event rather than any political gathering.</p>
                <p>I just don’t think this issue is going to go away with the conservatives and, of
                    course, it has adverse impact with the liberals too.</p>
                <p>With all due deference to Dr. Kissinger,
                    I believe that if détente is so fragile that it cannot stand a meeting with
                        Solzhenitsyn, it will fall on some
                    other account.<anchor n="3" xml:id="d165-f3"/></p>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d165-f1" n="1" type="source" xml:id="d165-f1-content">
                <p>Source: Ford Library, President’s Handwriting File, 1974–1977, Box 7,
                        Countries—USSR. No classification marking.
                        Ford initialed the memorandum; a
                    stamped note also indicates that he saw it.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d165-f2" n="2" xml:id="d165-f2-content">
                <p>Rudolf Nureyev, a Russian dancer, defected to the West in 1961; Mstislav
                    Rostropovich, a Russian cellist and conductor, emigrated to the United States in
                    1974.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d165-f3" n="3" xml:id="d165-f3-content">
                <p>On July 21, Solzhenitsyn rejected the
                    open invitation to meet with Ford at the
                    White House. He also criticized the President for planning to attend the
                    upcoming European Security Conference in Helsinki, which he called “the betrayal
                    of Eastern Europe.” (Bernard Gwertzman, “Solzhenitsyn Says Ford
                    Joins in Eastern Europe’s ‘Betrayal’,” <hi rendition="#italic">The New York
                        Times,</hi> July 22, 1975, pp. 1, 9)</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
